## 6. Backend Functional Design
## 6.1 Configuration and boot
The backend loads .env from project root. Important settings include:
- LLM_PROVIDER (gemini or ollama)
- Provider model names and endpoints
- FRONTEND_URL for CORS
- BACKEND_HOST and BACKEND_PORT
- SCHEMES_DATA_PATH

On startup:
- schemes.json is loaded into in-memory objects.
- SQLite tables are initialized if missing.
- API router is registered under /api.

Health endpoint:
- GET /health returns status and number of loaded schemes.

## 6.2 API surface
Primary endpoints include:
- POST /api/chat
  - Deterministic guided chat mode.
  - Returns assistant response, profile snapshot, evaluated schemes, metadata.

- GET /api/categories
  - Returns unique categories and counts.

- GET /api/schemes
  - Returns all schemes, optional category filter.

- GET /api/schemes/{scheme_id}
  - Returns detailed scheme object.

- GET /api/profile/{session_id}
  - Returns current profile state for session.

- POST /api/profile/{session_id}
  - Direct profile patch/update for allowed fields.

- POST /api/whatif
  - Simulates eligibility under hypothetical profile overrides.

- DELETE /api/session/{session_id}
  - Clears profile and conversation history for session.

- POST /api/ai-chat
  - Non-stream pure RAG assistant response.

- POST /api/ai-chat-stream
  - Streaming RAG assistant via text/event-stream style chunking.

Rate-limit and quota errors from LLM providers are explicitly mapped (notably 429 conditions).

## 6.3 Deterministic chat orchestration pipeline
The orchestrator is the core differentiator. For each user message, it executes:

1. Session state fetch
- Load profile and recent chat history.

2. LLM-based intent extraction
- Uses schema-guided extraction for intent, profile updates, query parameters.
- Cleans potential markdown wrappers around JSON.

3. Profile sanitization and merge
- Strict validations for age, income, class range, enums, BPL booleans, and basic name checks.
- Invalid/hallucinated extracted values are rejected.

4. Progressive eligibility run
- Eligibility is run once minimum context exists (income OR age+occupation OR explicit eligibility intent).
- Retrieval narrows candidate schemes.
- Scoring/ranking classifies into:
  - Confirmed eligible
  - Probably eligible (missing info)
  - Near-match/ineligible context

5. What-if simulation and scheme-detail handling
- If intent is what_if, applies temporary overrides and re-evaluates.
- If user asks for a specific scheme, finds best textual match and evaluates that scheme specifically.

6. Smart missing field strategy
- Selects only top priority missing fields based on user context and scheme interest.

7. Phase-aware response generation
Conversation phases include:
- greeting
- early_collection
- early_suggestion
- refined_results
- detail

LLM gets deterministic result context and explicit instructions to avoid contradiction.

8. Persistence
- Stores both user and assistant turns into conversation history.

9. Frontend response formatting
- Returns normalized scheme cards and metadata for UI rendering.

## 6.4 Eligibility engine behavior
The deterministic eligibility engine checks hard constraints such as:
- min_age/max_age/age_range
- income ceiling
- social category
- gender
- occupation
- farmer_type
- housing_status
- student_class
- marital_status
- special conditions context

Rule statuses:
- pass
- fail
- missing

Eligibility decision principle:
- Any fail means ineligible.
- Missing does not auto-fail; it is treated as potentially eligible pending data.

## 6.5 Scoring and ranking
Scoring combines:
- Hard-rule outcomes (pass/fail/missing).
- Soft constraints (example: BPL weight contributions).

Tiering logic prioritizes:
- Fully eligible (highest)
- Missing-data probable matches
- Ineligible results (lowest)

This creates a practical shortlist while preserving deterministic integrity.

## 6.6 Retrieval modules
There are two retrieval styles:

A) Deterministic retrieval engine
- Simple category + keyword match in name, Hindi name, description, tags, department.

B) RAG retrieval engine
- Builds tokenized index from all scheme fields.
- Uses BM25-inspired ranking over inverted index.
- Supports Hindi tokenization range and bilingual stopword filtering.
- Performs occupation and age compatibility checks for recommendation quality.
- Builds rich context blocks for the LLM.

## 6.7 Pure AI assistant (RAG mode)
The pure AI assistant:
- Extracts lightweight profile details from free text.
- Updates session profile when detectable.
- Retrieves top relevant schemes via RAG.
- In personalization scenarios, re-validates suggestions using deterministic eligibility checks.
- Streams chunked responses for responsive UX.
- Emits source cards to the frontend.

Prompt design enforces:
- Human officer persona.
- Hinglish style.
- Mandatory clickable scheme link format in responses:
  [[Scheme Name ->]](scheme_id)

## 6.8 Database and session model
SQLite has two main tables:
- user_profiles: session-level citizen profile fields.
- conversations: ordered user/assistant message history.

Important persistence properties:
- Profile upsert on each update.
- Conversation history fetched in chronological order from latest N messages.
- Session reset removes both profile and chat history.

## 7. Frontend Functional Design
## 7.1 UX positioning
The UI is designed as a modern, high-contrast, glassmorphism-inspired interface with:
- Distinct color identities for deterministic chat (orange-amber) and AI advisor (teal-cyan).
- Animated transitions and stagger effects.
- Mobile and desktop responsive layout behavior.

## 7.2 Main user journeys
1. Landing page
- Product overview, value proposition, and CTA.

2. Deterministic assistant (/chat)
- Session-based conversational interaction.
- Displays assistant response and eligible/probable scheme cards.
- Shows live profile completeness in sidebar.
- Supports aborting in-flight request and new session reset.

3. AI advisor (/ai)
- Streaming conversational response.
- Rich markdown-like rendering to HTML.
- Source chips for retrieved schemes.
- Supports clickable scheme links parsed from assistant output.

4. Manual exploration (/explore)
- Category browsing.
- Scheme card lists by category.
- Full scheme detail pages with eligibility criteria, documents, benefits, and application steps.

## 7.3 Language system
LanguageContext manages 3 languages:
- English
- Hindi
- Hinglish

It provides:
- translation map
- current language state
- t(key) helper
- language label for navbar and controls

## 7.4 AI response rendering and linking
The AI page contains a custom formatter that:
- Parses headings, bullets, ordered lists, bold/italic/code.
- Sanitizes links and scheme IDs.
- Converts scheme link syntax into internal clickable routes:
  /explore/scheme/{scheme_id}?from=ai

This enables a guided path from conversation to detailed scheme page.

## 8. Data Model and Scheme Knowledge
Each scheme object includes:
- identity and naming (English/Hindi)
- department and category
- description
- eligibility constraints (hard and soft)
- benefits (amount, type, frequency, descriptive text)
- required documents with mandatory/conditional metadata
- application process (online URL and offline steps)
- tags

Loaded once into memory for fast access and retrieval.

## 9. Runtime Behavior and Local Execution
The provided batch runner:
- Kills existing listeners on ports 8000 and 3000.
- Starts backend using virtualenv Python + uvicorn reload mode.
- Starts frontend via npm run dev.
- Opens browser to localhost:3000.

Expected endpoints while running:
- Frontend: http://localhost:3000
- Backend API: http://localhost:8000
- Health: http://localhost:8000/health

## 10. Safety, Trust, and Explainability Patterns
Trust-oriented design patterns in this codebase:
- Deterministic hard-rule engine for eligibility decisions.
- Separation between language generation and rule evaluation.
- Explicit profile sanitization against allowed enums and ranges.
- Missing-data handling to avoid false rejection.
- Explanatory metadata (failed rules, missing fields) for transparent decisions.

Potentially sensitive areas still requiring production hardening:
- Data-at-rest security and encryption.
- Session authentication/authorization model.
- LLM prompt injection hardening in open-input mode.
- Stricter HTML sanitization strategy for any rendered AI content.

## 11. Product Strengths
- Strong hybrid architecture: deterministic reliability plus AI usability.
- Progressive questioning and profile accumulation improve recommendation quality over time.
- Dual interaction modes: strict eligibility flow and open advisory flow.
- Useful manual exploration pathway independent of chat.
- Multilingual UX tuned for regional usage context.

## 12. Observed Constraints and Improvement Opportunities
Likely current constraints:
- In-memory and SQLite design suits local/prototype workloads more than large-scale concurrency.
- Rule coverage depends entirely on quality/completeness of schemes.json.
- Some heuristics (name extraction, fuzzy matching, tokenizer assumptions) can still misclassify edge cases.

High-value next improvements:
- Add automated tests for eligibility rules, sanitization, and phase transitions.
- Add schema/version checks for schemes dataset.
- Add admin tooling for scheme updates and validation workflows.
- Add authentication and secure session handling for public deployment.
- Add observability: structured logs, tracing, and metrics.

## 13. End-to-End Request Flow Summary
For deterministic mode:
- User sends message -> intent extraction -> profile update -> retrieval -> rule engine -> ranked outcomes -> phase-based response generation -> persistence -> UI cards.

For RAG mode:
- User message -> retrieval index query -> relevance filtering -> optional deterministic re-check for personalization -> streamed narrative output + sources -> persistence.

This design intentionally keeps decision correctness anchored in deterministic code while using AI for natural conversation quality.

## 14. Conclusion
Samarth is a well-structured, hybrid AI government-scheme assistant tailored for Jharkhand. It combines deterministic eligibility evaluation with conversational intelligence and a clean multi-page frontend experience. The architecture clearly prioritizes explainability, local operability, and multilingual accessibility, making it a solid dissertation-grade applied AI system with direct civic utility.
